</div>

<div class="section-header">Dining Out Playbook</div>

<table>
  <tr><th>Type of Place</th><th>Order This</th><th>Skip This</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Israeli / Mediterranean</td><td>Hummus, baba, tabbouleh, fattoush, grilled vegetables, lentil soup, grilled fish, salads with olive oil &amp; lemon</td><td>Shawarma, kebab, schnitzel, fried falafel, cheese-stuffed anything</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Italian</td><td>Whole-wheat pasta primavera, pasta e fagioli, minestrone, grilled fish, big salad, marinara-based</td><td>Cream sauces, cheese-heavy, pepperoni, sausage, butter-based, fried calamari</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Japanese</td><td>Edamame, seaweed salad, miso soup, sashimi, brown-rice rolls, vegetable rolls</td><td>Tempura, deep-fried, eel sauce</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Steakhouse / American</td><td>Big salad start, grilled fish, double vegetables, baked potato (no butter — ask for olive oil)</td><td>Steak, butter, cheese plate, dessert</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Hotel breakfast</td><td>Oatmeal + berries + nuts, fresh fruit, whole-grain toast with tahini, coffee</td><td>Bacon, sausage, omelette with cheese, pastries, white toast</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="callout">
  <b>One rule that handles most restaurants:</b> "Mostly plants, no fried, no cream, no melted cheese." Say it to the waiter, they'll figure it out.
</div>

<div class="section-header">The Phase-In — Don't Try to Do This All Monday</div>

<table>
  <tr><th>Week</th><th>Add</th><th>Drop</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Week 1–2</td><td>Beans/lentils every day. 6 servings greens.</td><td>Red meat. Soda. Donuts.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Week 3–4</td><td>Whole grains only (no white). Plant milk in coffee.</td><td>Processed deli meat. Pastries. White bread.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Week 5–6</td><td>Hummus and tahini-based dressings replace mayo and butter.</td><td>Dairy. Fried foods entirely.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Week 7–8</td><td>Settle into 5 favorite breakfasts, 5 lunches, 5 dinners.</td><td>Anything still drifting in from the old menu.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Maintenance</td><td>Reassess labs at 3 and 6 months — LDL, ApoB, hs-CRP, A1c.</td><td>Anything the labs say isn't working.</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="section-header">What to Track Daily / Weekly</div>

<ul>
  <li><b>Labs at 3 months and 6 months:</b> LDL (target &lt;55), ApoB (target &lt;60), hs-CRP, A1c, fasting glucose, fasting insulin</li>
  <li><b>Body:</b> Weight weekly. Blood pressure 2× per week (morning).</li>
  <li><b>Daily checklist:</b> 6 servings of greens? Beans? 30 min movement?</li>
  <li><b>Symptoms to take SERIOUSLY:</b> chest pressure, shortness of breath, jaw/arm pain on exertion, dizziness, palpitations — call cardiology same day. Don't wait.</li>
</ul>

<div class="green-callout">
  <b>Realistic expectations.</b> This diet doesn't dissolve plaque overnight. What it reliably does: drops LDL 15–30%, drops ApoB, drops hs-CRP (inflammation), drops blood pressure, drops A1c. The plaque you already have gets <i>stabilized</i> — meaning it doesn't rupture. <b>Stabilized plaque doesn't rupture, and ruptured plaque is what causes heart attacks.</b> That's the actual win.
</div>

<div class="red-callout">
  <b>This is not a substitute for your statin.</b> Post-stent, lifestyle alone is undertreatment. Diet drops LDL ~15–30%. A high-intensity statin drops it another 50%. You need both. If you're not on Rosuvastatin 20–40 or Atorvastatin 40–80, that's the first conversation with your cardiologist.
</div>

